plicit stress handling info. Leadership may offset some teamwork, but unfriendly may hinder crew cooperation. Health concerns could affect long flights.\n\nB: Strong stress handling, conscientious, good weather assessment, computer skills. But interpersonal issues: pretentious, wrong tone, nasty remarks. Those could harm crew cooperation. No explicit teamwork but negative hints.\n\nC: Quick correct decisions, excellent attention, puts concern for others above everything (good teamwork), but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education (long-term development). Mixed: good decision-making, but communication issues and egocentric may affect crew. Not willing to further education may limit growth.\n\nD: Responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates, solves problems, takes responsibility. But moody, loner (teamwork issues). No explicit stress handling but responds adequately. Good problem solving and responsibility. Moodiness and loner may affect crew.\n\nWe need to weigh which candidate overall best. Probably B has strong stress handling but interpersonal issues severe. C has good decision-making and concern for others, but communication difficulty and egocentric. D has responsibility and problem solving but moody and loner. A has technical and leadership but unfriendly and health.\n\nWhich is best? For long-distance flights, stress handling and teamwork are crucial. B handles stress well but interpersonal issues may be fatal. C's communication difficulty could be problematic. D's moodiness could affect long flights. A's health and unfriendly may be problematic.\n\nMaybe C is best because despite communication difficulty, he puts concern for others above everything, which may mitigate egocentric? But difficulty communicating ideas is a big issue for crew coordination. Egocentric also negative.\n\nMaybe B's stress handling outweighs interpersonal? But nasty remarks could cause crew conflict.\n\nD seems balanced: good problem solvi